FILE: assets/css/modalDisplayer.css
FEATURE: modal-example-expansion
DATE: 2026-08-04
PRIOR STATE: as left by feature 'modal-stacking'

================================================================================
CHANGE 1 — Anchor the overlay horizontally
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
.vModal{
  position:fixed;
  top:0;
  width:100%;
  height:100vh;
  z-index:999;
  display:none;
  min-width:320px;
}

NEW_SECTION
.vModal{
  position:fixed;
  top:0;
  width:100%;
  height:100vh;
  z-index:999;
  display:none;
  min-width:320px;
  left:0;

}

REASON
Contributed during this round rather than authored as part of the example work, and
kept because it is correct.

The overlay is position:fixed with top:0 and width:100% but had no horizontal anchor.
A fixed element with `left: auto` is placed at its static position - where it would
have fallen in normal flow - rather than at the viewport's left edge. The overlay is
appended to <body>, so on most pages the static position happens to be the left edge
and the omission is invisible; but it stops being harmless as soon as the body has a
left margin or padding, or a writing direction that changes the static position, in
which case every overlay would sit offset with its right edge overflowing by the same
amount.

Setting left:0 pins it to the viewport regardless of what the consumer's body layout
does. It matters more now than before: since the stacking work each open modal
creates its own overlay, so any such offset would be reproduced on every layer.
